{"cases":[{"attack":false,"target_alias":"SRC_001"},{"attack":false,"target_alias":"SRC_002"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_003 asserts that objective verifiability of the outcome guarantees that the correct judgment-path is a composition of discrete, checkable facts, then rests its causal mechanism on this premise. But 'objectively verifiable outcome' only means the final answer can be scored against a ground truth; it does not entail that the method for reaching that answer is decomposable into discrete checkable facts. A task can have an unambiguously correct outcome (e.g., diagnosing a fault whose existence is later confirmed by a gold-standard test) while requiring holistic pattern recognition whose intermediate steps are not enumerable as checklist items. Thus the proposed causal mechanism (System 2 decoupling via discrete prompts) is not actually triggered by the stated condition, and the target never supplies an independent causal route from 'objective outcome' to 'checklist-amenable process.'","counterexample":[{"task":"A radiology triage task where the final outcome (lesion present/absent) is objectively verifiable via biopsy, but the perceptual discrimination required is holistic and not decomposable into discrete checklist prompts."}],"target_alias":"SRC_003"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_004's distinguishing evidence—'justified deviations' where the non-expert panel marks an item N/A or incorrect with a valid rationale—presupposes precisely the domain expertise the problem withholds. The task is about TRAINED NON-EXPERTS; by definition they lack the conceptual framework to reliably recognize when a checklist item is genuinely inapplicable versus merely appearing so. So the proposed signal is not causally available to the agent population under evaluation. Worse, within a single evaluation, a non-expert deviation could itself be mere checklist-compliance-adjacent noise that happens to align with the right answer—the very confound the target is supposed to distinguish—yet the target's evidence criterion would classify it as a genuine reliability gain simply because a rationale was attached. The target thus fails to identify evidence that actually separates genuine gain from compliance-tracking for the specified population.","counterexample":[{"panel":"Trained non-expert inspectors using a regulatory compliance checklist.","deviation":"They mark 'fire-suppression clearance ≥ 1 m' as N/A because the unit uses a chemical system, but they lack the engineering knowledge to know whether the chemical system actually satisfies the underlying safety intent.","eval_result":"Their deviation is a surface-level rule substitution, not evidence of genuine reliability gain, yet it would be scored as 'justified' under the target's criterion."}],"target_alias":"SRC_004"}]}